# novadeck INSTALLER image — the declared package set. Phase 6 of
# .claude/plans/internal-install.plan.md.
#
# Read by install/mkroot.sh (which lays the root down from it) and by `make relock-installer`
# (which re-resolves it against the pinned snapshot and writes install/manifest.lock). It is the
# human-editable DECLARATION of intent; the lock is the resolved closure. Edit here, relock, read
# the diff — exactly the loop images/customize-base.sh's PKGS array has with images/manifest.lock.
#
# THIS IS NOT THE SHIPPED IMAGE'S SET AND MUST NOT DRIFT TOWARDS IT. The installer is a tool that
# runs as root against a stranger's internal disk and then goes away. It carries no Steam, no
# `deck` user, no SDDM, no session, no Bluetooth, no audio and no FEX. What it carries is: enough
# systemd to boot, the filesystem and partition tools the spine calls by name, RAUC to write the
# slot, a network stack for §4b, and the Phase-5 display stack so the consent screen can be drawn.
#
# EVERY NAME BELOW IS A LEAF. Dependency closures are pacman's job and land in the lock; declaring
# a closure by hand is how a set drifts from what actually resolves. gamescope alone pulls sdl2,
# xorg-server-xwayland, libinput, pipewire and ~20 X libraries through its own depends.
#
# One name per line. `#` comments and blank lines are ignored.

# ---- the foundation ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The same vendor metapackage the shipped image bootstraps from, for the same reason: it reproduces
# upstream's own minimal set (systemd + udev for device nodes and unit ordering, journald for the
# log save-log.sh pulls off the medium, bash/coreutils/util-linux, tar, mount) instead of a
# hand-reconstructed list that would drift on every snapshot bump. It drags pacman and
# archlinux-keyring in as dependencies; unlike the release image there is no seal here that removes
# them, and that is deliberate — an installer with a package manager on it is a diagnosable
# installer, and nothing about this image is sealed or measured after the fact.
base

# ---- what the spine calls BY NAME ----------------------------------------------------------------
# novadeck-install's recon() checks each of these at second zero, before the carve, precisely
# because a missing tool discovered later costs the user their Android data to reach an error that
# was always knowable. Four of them are absent from the SHIPPED image, which is why
# install/lib-hwstage.sh exists to stage them onto a dev card — see that file, and
# [[offline-suite-inherits-host-path]]: an offline suite inherits the HOST's PATH, so `make test`
# being green says nothing at all about this list.
gptfdisk       # sgdisk — reads and writes the GPT; select-target.sh and carve.sh refuse without it
dosfstools     # mkfs.vfat/mkfs.fat — the shared ESP and both efi partitions
mtools         # mdir — reads a FOREIGN ESP's content, which is how rule 3b recognises another
               #   distro's install. NOT optional and NOT covered by dosfstools: leaving it out
               #   reproduces the 2026-08-21 Pocket FIT finding (the gate failed OPEN) on this image
parted         # partprobe — re-reads the table after the carve; recon() requires it outright
e2fsprogs      # mkfs.ext4 — /home
btrfs-progs    # mkfs.btrfs, btrfstune, btrfs — the root and var slots
zstd           # the published steam seed is a .tar.zst and GNU tar shells out to this binary
curl           # fetches the bundle and the seed
ca-certificates  # ...over https

# ---- writing the slot ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# rauc-session.sh starts a PRIVATE session bus and drives rauc over it, so both halves ship: the
# daemon binary (dbus) and the client the spine talks with (gdbus, from glib2). glib2 arrives as a
# rauc dependency anyway; it is declared because the spine calls `gdbus` by name, and a leaf that
# is only present transitively is a leaf that can vanish in a bump without a dependency error.
rauc
dbus
glib2

# ---- network (§4b) -------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# install/netcfg drives NetworkManager with nmcli. wireless-regdb is REQUIRED for 5 GHz (without it
# those channels are no-TX), which on a device being installed over Wi-Fi is the difference between
# a 4 GB bundle arriving and not. openssh is here for the same reason the fallback console is: when
# the panel shows nothing, a shell is the only way in on boards with no UART ([[sm8650-no-uart]]).
networkmanager
wpa_supplicant
wireless-regdb
openssh

# ---- the Phase-5 display stack -------------------------------------------------------------------
# gamescope as ROOT via seatd-launch, with install/ui as its only client — the shape
# docs/bringup-phase2.md gates 1b/1c validated on hardware, and the one HW-proven on a Pocket S2 on
# 2026-08-22. NOT SDL2-on-KMSDRM: every display fight on these panels was won against the gamescope
# path, and KMSDRM pulls GBM/EGL/mesa anyway so the size argument for it never existed.
#
# NO python-pygame AND NO PySDL2 — measured against the pinned builder, neither exists in the holo
# repos (there is not even a plain `sdl2`, only sdl2-compat/sdl2_ttf/sdl3). The SDL binding arrives
# as install/pygame-ce.pin, a wheel that vendors its own libSDL2 with the wayland driver compiled
# in; see that pin for why a vendored wheel was the acceptable answer here.
#
# NO SDDM. It is a login manager, its greeter is never started, and what it buys the main image (an
# active seat0 logind session so stock polkit authorizes Wi-Fi) is meaningless for a tool that is
# already root. NO PLYMOUTH either — plymouthd starves gamescope of DRM master
# ([[boot-splash-plymouth]]); the installer boots to a black panel for a few seconds instead.
mesa
vulkan-icd-loader
vulkan-freedreno
wayland
gamescope
seatd
python           # install/ui and its three model files; the wheel above is cp313, so this pins 3.13

# ---- InputPlumber's runtime dependency -------------------------------------------------------------
# InputPlumber itself is not a repo package: it is packages/inputplumber/prebuilt.pin, a sha256'd
# tarball that install/mkroot.sh places by name (the release bootstrap auto-discovers every
# packages/*/prebuilt.pin; this image takes exactly two, and pygame-ce is the other). It ships here
# NOT for size but so the installer's input path is IDENTICAL to the HW-validated main-image one,
# rather than a second raw-evdev stack inside the tool that has to work when the device is broken.
# libiio is the dep its own pin declares (it links libiio.so.0), so it is a leaf of THIS list.
libiio
